{"Setting a placeholder refresh token overwrites the stored real refresh token whenever the response omits it (e.g., when credential sharing is enabled), making future refreshes impossible if the feature is later disabled. Should preserve the existing refresh token instead.": 0.9274804592132568, "Dynamic keys in minimumTokenResponseSchema using [z.string().toString()] evaluate to a fixed literal key (likely '[object Object]'), causing any real token response to fail validation and throw on refresh. Should use a permissive schema with .passthrough() or .catchall(z.unknown()).": 0.921207845211029, "parseRefreshTokenResponse returns a Zod safeParse result ({ success, data }), but it's stored directly into the credential key, writing the wrapper object { success: true, data: {...} } to the DB instead of the actual credential, corrupting it. Should access .data before storing.": 0.9339891672134399, "In sync mode refreshOAuthTokens returns a fetch Response, while in non-sync mode it returns whatever refreshFunction returns (often an Axios response or token object). Callers like Zoho/HubSpot expect .data and will crash when a Response is returned. Return type should be consistent.": 0.9382747411727905, "Response.statusText is often an empty string for successful HTTP responses (status 200) in Node.js fetch, so the condition '' !== 'OK' evaluates to true, causing HttpError to be thrown even for successful responses. Should use response.ok or status code check instead.": 0.9133726358413696, "Webhook handler doesn't enforce HTTP method and parse will throw on invalid/empty bodies, leading to a 500 for GET/invalid input. Should add method guard and use safeParse to return 400 instead.": 0.9643716216087341, "JSON.parse(symmetricDecrypt(...)) can throw on invalid ciphertext or JSON, bubbling up as a 500 on externally-facing webhook. Should wrap in try/catch and return 400 on failure.": 0.9647537469863892}